{"chunk_id": "050756025b20", "text": "Bhishma recounts to Yudhishthira a philosophical encounter between King Janaka of Mithila and the wandering yogini Sulabha. Janaka claims that true emancipation (moksha) comes from knowledge and inner renunciation rather than outward abandonment of worldly roles; thus he rules a kingdom while remaining inwardly detached. Suspecting his claim, Sulabha uses yogic power to enter his consciousness and test him. Janaka rebukes her, accusing her of impropriety and questioning her motives. Sulabha responds with a sophisticated discourse on speech, knowledge, the senses, and the composite nature of the body and mind. She argues that identity, caste, and ownership are illusions produced by the body\u2019s changing elements, and challenges Janaka\u2019s supposed detachment by exposing the many dependencies and anxieties inherent in kingship. The debate explores whether liberation lies in external renunciation or true inner knowledge of the Self.\nBhishma recounts the debate between King Janaka and the yogini Sulabha on true emancipation, knowledge, renunciation, and the nature of self and perception.\nThemes: true renunciation vs outward asceticism, knowledge and liberation, self and consciousness, philosophical debate, social roles and spirituality", "vector": [0.021588312461972237, -0.0009217781480401754, 0.01167585700750351, -0.01080083567649126, -0.03486726060509682, 0.014441191218793392, -0.02104058861732483, -0.0036804331466555595, -0.027493035420775414, -0.02502160146832466, 0.007821755483746529, 0.023391790688037872, 0.00719387736171484, 0.01990506425499916, -0.020760048180818558, -0.016592007130384445, 0.05760445445775986, -0.013325706124305725, 0.013439258560538292, -0.012991728261113167, 0.007307429797947407, -0.02396623231470585, 0.002745295874774456, 0.009331334382295609, -0.00615520728752017, 0.021574953570961952, 0.026811720803380013, -0.022389858961105347, -0.00698681129142642, -0.01959780603647232, -0.010152919217944145, 